      "writtenOn": "2019-11-13T21:10:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`-j` seems to be a global option, and each `-f file` is prefixed with the last `-P` argument, so we need to reset `-P` between each file.\n\nFor example:\n\n#1 - clearly without a second `-P`, \u0027b\u0027 will respect the `-P` from before, and be under `1/b`:\n$ soong_zip -o out.zip -j   -P 1 -f a   -f b\n$ unzip -l out.zip\nArchive:  out.zip\n  Length      Date    Time    Name\n---------  ---------- -----   ----\n        0  2008-01-01 00:00   1/a\n        0  2008-01-01 00:00   1/b\n---------                     -------\n        0                     2 files\n\n#2 - even if we `-j` a second time, it respects the `-P` from before\n$ soong_zip -o out.zip -j   -P 1 -f a    -j -f b\nr$ unzip -l out.zip\nArchive:  out.zip\n  Length      Date    Time    Name\n---------  ---------- -----   ----\n        0  2008-01-01 00:00   1/a\n        0  2008-01-01 00:00   1/b\n---------                     -------\n        0                     2 files\n\n#3 - the only way to fix is provide something to `-P` that evaluates to empty string...\n$ soong_zip -o out.zip -j    -P 1 -f a    -P \u0027\u0027 -f b\n$ unzip -l out.zip\nArchive:  out.zip\n  Length      Date    Time    Name\n---------  ---------- -----   ----\n        0  2008-01-01 00:00   1/a\n        0  2008-01-01 00:00   b\n---------                     -------\n        0                     2 files\n\n#4 - and a blank -P argument isn\u0027t sufficient :(\n$ soong_zip -o out.zip -j    -P 1 -f a    -P -f b\n    unexpected arguments b\n\n... so we need to reset `-P` to something blank between each file. I\u0027ve done this via checking strlen() so users don\u0027t have to do the `\"\u0027\u0027\"` nonsense, we just handle it gracefully later. LGTY?",
      "parentUuid": "fd28af38_9e608d50",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 291,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 291,
        "endChar": 8
      },
